1
00:00:00,671 --> 00:00:10,756
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to talk about the theme of Brahma-vihāra, but starting with the Jīvaka Sutta, Majjhimikaya, 55.

2
00:00:12,397 --> 00:00:13,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay?

3
00:00:14,899 --> 00:00:19,101
[SPEAKER_01]: And, I'll just read a section, a few sections.

4
00:00:21,262 --> 00:00:28,366
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha says to Jīvaka, Jīvaka, I say that there are three instances in which meat should not be eaten.

5
00:00:29,214 --> 00:00:38,559
[SPEAKER_01]: When it is seen, heard or suspected that the living being has been slaughtered for oneself, I say that meat should not be eaten in these three instances.

6
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:49,165
[SPEAKER_01]: I say that there are three instances in which meat may be eaten when it is not seen, not heard and not suspected that the living being has been slaughtered for oneself.

7
00:00:50,366 --> 00:00:54,288
[SPEAKER_01]: I say that meat may be eaten in these three instances.

8
00:00:56,850 --> 00:00:58,751
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is

9
00:01:00,470 --> 00:01:15,820
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the question is, yeah, if a person who is cultivating dharmavihāra practice, training in that practice, or developing, or developed in that practice, can he eat meat?

10
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:17,581
[SPEAKER_01]: And the answer is yes.

11
00:01:18,541 --> 00:01:19,982
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what he asked Buddha initially.

12
00:01:20,002 --> 00:01:22,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, that's, that's... What is the implication of that?

13
00:01:22,364 --> 00:01:22,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

14
00:01:22,564 --> 00:01:23,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

15
00:01:24,845 --> 00:01:28,608
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a person who is cultivating things like kindness,

16
00:01:29,627 --> 00:01:32,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, of course.

17
00:01:32,891 --> 00:01:34,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

18
00:01:47,193 --> 00:01:54,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Just a little bit aside, as a lay person, buying meat from a supermarket, you don't directly kill anything.

19
00:01:54,977 --> 00:01:55,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right.

20
00:01:55,517 --> 00:02:00,339
[SPEAKER_01]: But you still, you go and you desire meat, you go for it, you buy it.

21
00:02:00,859 --> 00:02:01,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that blameless?

22
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:03,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Or blameworthy?

23
00:02:04,481 --> 00:02:06,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

24
00:02:06,782 --> 00:02:11,484
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know for sure, the animal hasn't been killed for you.

25
00:02:12,865 --> 00:02:12,965
[SPEAKER_00]: And

26
00:02:13,535 --> 00:02:14,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just that.

27
00:02:14,216 --> 00:02:16,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Probably, that meat is not sold.

28
00:02:16,118 --> 00:02:17,099
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be thrown away.

29
00:02:18,321 --> 00:02:21,104
[SPEAKER_00]: But the same amount of animals will be slaughtered.

30
00:02:22,686 --> 00:02:26,871
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it makes no difference otherwise, whether you buy it or not.

31
00:02:27,452 --> 00:02:30,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually, the argument is buying the meat supposes the meat industry.

32
00:02:31,673 --> 00:02:35,455
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's so far removed from any practical basis that it's not true.

33
00:02:36,795 --> 00:02:48,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that argument is based on that assumption that we are all equal, that we are all the same, that we all make same choices, that we could all make same choices.

34
00:02:49,381 --> 00:02:50,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's impossible.

35
00:02:50,950 --> 00:02:53,352
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible for all humans to make same choices.

36
00:02:53,632 --> 00:02:54,733
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just inconceivable.

37
00:02:55,114 --> 00:02:56,535
[SPEAKER_00]: But in theory, you can conceive it.

38
00:02:57,175 --> 00:03:07,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in your mind, like, if you stop... like, even if you stop buying meat for the rest of your life, and you spend every single day of your life educating people to not buy meat, you wouldn't make a slightest dent in the meat industry.

39
00:03:08,425 --> 00:03:11,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Because how many billion people are there on Earth?

40
00:03:12,028 --> 00:03:13,068
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely no way.

41
00:03:13,089 --> 00:03:14,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to recognize that people are different.

42
00:03:15,757 --> 00:03:22,225
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to not eat meat or not buy meat, you cannot do it for those kind of abstract projected reasons that are not accurate.

43
00:03:22,626 --> 00:03:24,067
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to find internal reasons.

44
00:03:25,649 --> 00:03:26,951
[SPEAKER_00]: And there are plenty to find for.

45
00:03:26,991 --> 00:03:33,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, most of the... like, mass-produced meat is full of hormones and the way animals are killed, meat is full of adrenaline and all sorts of stuff.

46
00:03:35,737 --> 00:03:37,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those should be your reasons if you don't want to buy it.

47
00:03:38,398 --> 00:03:46,160
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you cannot not buy it, if there is nothing else, if it's the cheapest, if you, like, need that food, then it's fine.

48
00:03:48,881 --> 00:03:50,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but you say, if you need it.

49
00:03:50,941 --> 00:03:51,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

50
00:03:51,962 --> 00:03:53,102
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the...

51
00:03:53,562 --> 00:03:54,923
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the... What is the...

52
00:03:54,963 --> 00:03:55,923
[SPEAKER_01]: But the term is the need.

53
00:03:56,703 --> 00:03:58,244
[SPEAKER_01]: But what is the blameworthy?

54
00:03:58,644 --> 00:03:59,984
[SPEAKER_01]: What is blameworthy there?

55
00:04:01,083 --> 00:04:01,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Where?

56
00:04:01,724 --> 00:04:03,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that blameless?

57
00:04:03,185 --> 00:04:06,288
[SPEAKER_01]: If we just said it's completely... What?

58
00:04:06,468 --> 00:04:06,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

59
00:04:06,668 --> 00:04:06,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one?

60
00:04:07,209 --> 00:04:12,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Buying food that you... Like, you could... you could choose chickpeas.

61
00:04:13,274 --> 00:04:14,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right, right.

62
00:04:14,536 --> 00:04:17,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not a moral question of blameworthy or non-blameworthy.

63
00:04:17,578 --> 00:04:18,880
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like ethical question.

64
00:04:20,441 --> 00:04:21,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's up.

65
00:04:21,422 --> 00:04:23,184
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can refrain from it, don't do it.

66
00:04:23,284 --> 00:04:25,626
[SPEAKER_00]: If you... if you... if it's too much

67
00:04:27,208 --> 00:04:32,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Strain, or the other aspects of your life, you have to focus on not eating this, and you need more money, and whatever else.

68
00:04:34,434 --> 00:04:38,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can't get any other form of protein, or it's too expensive, and so on.

69
00:04:38,477 --> 00:04:41,359
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not optional.

70
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:49,326
[SPEAKER_00]: But as long as you are not supporting the killing, by ordering me to be killed, by knowing that it's being killed for you,

71
00:04:52,056 --> 00:04:52,596
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

72
00:04:52,636 --> 00:04:53,476
[SPEAKER_00]: It's so far removed.

73
00:04:54,016 --> 00:04:58,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, again, theoretically, you would think, yes, but we should all act from that ideal.

74
00:04:58,877 --> 00:05:05,959
[SPEAKER_00]: But not if that ideal is divorced from reality, such as people are not the same, and it's impossible to make everybody to make same decisions.

75
00:05:06,119 --> 00:05:07,179
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just inconceivable.

76
00:05:09,119 --> 00:05:21,242
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if for fact, there will be people they will be killing regardless, if you come across what's already killed had nothing to do with you, like, oh, well, should you feel guilty in partaking in it?

77
00:05:21,422 --> 00:05:21,602
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

78
00:05:23,022 --> 00:05:28,684
[SPEAKER_00]: You can if you want to, but even so, it would be... the guilt should be based on ethical reasons, not on moral reasons.

79
00:05:30,104 --> 00:05:31,805
[SPEAKER_01]: In the suttas, I'll read further.

80
00:05:33,065 --> 00:05:41,308
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems to suggest to me that there is a... that that would still be blameworthy to a certain degree, because of certain factors.

81
00:05:42,768 --> 00:05:44,148
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not quite sure what you're talking about now.

82
00:05:44,168 --> 00:05:44,849
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll read further.

83
00:05:44,869 --> 00:05:45,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, okay.

84
00:05:45,649 --> 00:05:48,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the Buddha explains what is truly blameless.

85
00:05:48,790 --> 00:05:49,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

86
00:05:51,611 --> 00:05:52,491
[SPEAKER_01]: In terms of eating meat.

87
00:05:57,519 --> 00:06:04,064
[SPEAKER_01]: He continues to say, here, Jīvaka, some bhikkhu lives in dependence upon a certain village or town.

88
00:06:05,766 --> 00:06:15,775
[SPEAKER_01]: He lives including one section of his experience within a benevolent mind, which he has discerned.

89
00:06:17,076 --> 00:06:17,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Likewise,

90
00:06:19,117 --> 00:06:21,159
[SPEAKER_01]: The second section of his experience.

91
00:06:21,259 --> 00:06:22,140
[SPEAKER_01]: The third section.

92
00:06:22,360 --> 00:06:22,780
[SPEAKER_01]: The fourth.

93
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:24,602
[SPEAKER_01]: So, above, below, around and everywhere.

94
00:06:24,862 --> 00:06:25,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Section or direction?

95
00:06:26,123 --> 00:06:26,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Direction.

96
00:06:27,844 --> 00:06:29,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Section of his experience.

97
00:06:29,065 --> 00:06:29,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

98
00:06:30,026 --> 00:06:30,607
[SPEAKER_01]: One direction.

99
00:06:31,427 --> 00:06:31,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Everywhere.

100
00:06:32,941 --> 00:06:36,923
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all directions of his experience, he includes within his benevolent mind.

101
00:06:37,043 --> 00:06:37,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

102
00:06:37,663 --> 00:06:41,265
[SPEAKER_01]: All to... as to... and to all as to himself.

103
00:06:41,885 --> 00:06:53,071
[SPEAKER_01]: He lives, including the whole world, within a benevolent mind, which is abundant, exalted, immeasurable, without hostility, and without ill will.

104
00:06:57,773 --> 00:06:58,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he...

105
00:06:59,373 --> 00:07:01,774
[SPEAKER_01]: He practices that way.

106
00:07:01,854 --> 00:07:02,694
[SPEAKER_01]: He trains that way.

107
00:07:02,914 --> 00:07:03,634
[SPEAKER_01]: He lives like that.

108
00:07:04,395 --> 00:07:06,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's maybe someone who's already developed that.

109
00:07:08,196 --> 00:07:12,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, a householder comes to him and invites him for the next day's meal.

110
00:07:13,077 --> 00:07:18,039
[SPEAKER_01]: The bhikkhu accepts if he likes.

111
00:07:18,059 --> 00:07:25,881
[SPEAKER_01]: When the night is ended, in the morning, he dresses and takes his bowl and outer robe, goes to the house of that householder and sits down on a seat made ready.

112
00:07:26,701 --> 00:07:29,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Then the householder serves him with good alms food.

113
00:07:31,973 --> 00:07:38,256
[SPEAKER_01]: He does not think, that bhikkhu does not think how good that the householder serves me with the good alms food.

114
00:07:39,356 --> 00:07:43,318
[SPEAKER_01]: If only a householder might serve me with such good alms food in the future.

115
00:07:45,178 --> 00:07:46,439
[SPEAKER_01]: He does not think thus.

116
00:07:47,239 --> 00:07:56,103
[SPEAKER_01]: He eats the alms food without being tired to it, infatuated with it and utterly committed to it, seeing the danger in it and understanding the escape from it.

117
00:07:57,498 --> 00:07:59,179
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you think, Jīvaka?

118
00:07:59,579 --> 00:08:06,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Would that bhikkhu, on such an occasion, choose for his own affliction, or for another's affliction, or for the affliction of both?

119
00:08:07,845 --> 00:08:08,645
[SPEAKER_01]: No, venerable sir.

120
00:08:09,566 --> 00:08:13,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Does not that bhikkhu sustain himself with blameless food on that occasion?

121
00:08:14,309 --> 00:08:15,290
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, that's what I'm saying.

122
00:08:15,550 --> 00:08:20,433
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what he's saying, is that that is the only blameless way of partaking.

123
00:08:20,473 --> 00:08:21,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, I see.

124
00:08:21,053 --> 00:08:23,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it has nothing to do with meat, specifically.

125
00:08:25,012 --> 00:08:25,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

126
00:08:25,852 --> 00:08:32,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but... No, no, I'm just saying, because the first question was simply about, oh, meat, non-meat, can you eat it or not, based on what you have available.

127
00:08:33,035 --> 00:08:36,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like one topic, but now you're talking about food in general.

128
00:08:36,817 --> 00:08:40,419
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I start with that question, can a person who...

129
00:08:40,439 --> 00:08:47,702
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is, it's like you can abstain from eating meat all you want, you'll be equally blameworthy for eating with your desire and for wrong reasons.

130
00:08:49,583 --> 00:08:57,608
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you... and if you've done it for wrong reasons, it's impossible for you to be blameworthy, even if you buy all the meat in the world.

131
00:08:58,637 --> 00:09:00,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I was saying.

132
00:09:00,418 --> 00:09:03,279
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I think is slightly blameworthy.

133
00:09:04,199 --> 00:09:15,202
[SPEAKER_01]: If you, if you, if you've got a mind of desire for that meat, for that taste, not because you need it and that's the only resource you have, which can only get there, it's the cheapest thing.

134
00:09:15,522 --> 00:09:16,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course.

135
00:09:16,483 --> 00:09:19,564
[SPEAKER_01]: That is blameworthy in the sense of your desires.

136
00:09:19,844 --> 00:09:21,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

137
00:09:21,404 --> 00:09:22,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what makes it blameworthy.

138
00:09:22,865 --> 00:09:23,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

139
00:09:24,125 --> 00:09:26,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Or killing specifically for yourself, sure.

140
00:09:26,586 --> 00:09:33,948
[SPEAKER_00]: But once that's not involved, so you know it's not killed for you, you don't suspect it's killed for you, and so on, then your desire is blameworthy.

141
00:09:33,988 --> 00:09:35,588
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, you're not talking about the meat anymore.

142
00:09:35,688 --> 00:09:36,768
[SPEAKER_00]: You're talking about food in general.

143
00:09:37,028 --> 00:09:37,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

144
00:09:37,749 --> 00:09:45,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Any food you eat with that desire, for a wrong reason, not for... not for sustenance, not out of the established mind, it will be blameworthy.

145
00:09:48,351 --> 00:09:51,292
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what I find is interesting is that, I mean,

146
00:09:56,163 --> 00:10:14,854
[SPEAKER_01]: The practice of that peaceful mind, that peaceful abiding, benevolence, is not... is... how he says... one doesn't... is not tied to that food, infatuated with, utterly committed to it.

147
00:10:14,934 --> 00:10:20,457
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no desire for that thing to... that households to bring me good food, to get food.

148
00:10:21,918 --> 00:10:24,040
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the practice of peacefulness.

149
00:10:24,121 --> 00:10:24,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

150
00:10:24,401 --> 00:10:25,682
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the practice of neverliness.

151
00:10:25,802 --> 00:10:32,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, practice of metta, friendliness, eh, if done correctly, establishes your mind in dispassion.

152
00:10:33,892 --> 00:10:34,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

153
00:10:34,925 --> 00:10:40,629
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there's no basis for passion, you cannot possibly exercise thoughts of, hmm, I wish he brings me this food.

154
00:10:40,689 --> 00:10:45,592
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just there is no passion for any food, any experience, because of the mind of friendliness.

155
00:10:46,653 --> 00:10:48,694
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not trying to get something from anyone else.

156
00:10:48,714 --> 00:10:49,975
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

157
00:10:50,055 --> 00:10:50,715
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

158
00:10:51,035 --> 00:10:57,340
[SPEAKER_00]: All you do is protect the framework of friendliness, of benevolence, of non-harm.

159
00:10:58,941 --> 00:10:59,441
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that

160
00:11:00,721 --> 00:11:02,741
[SPEAKER_00]: This implies absence of passion.

161
00:11:03,021 --> 00:11:05,002
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why those states are wholesome.

162
00:11:08,122 --> 00:11:23,785
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, although you're not specifically sending your metta to food, you will be unable to eat it with greed and desire, because the mind is established upon metta, properly, upon karuṇa, properly.

163
00:11:24,565 --> 00:11:27,286
[SPEAKER_00]: So there's no basis for any passion, regardless what a passion is.

164
00:11:35,436 --> 00:11:43,687
[SPEAKER_00]: But the important thing there is what you said in the beginning, when you read the passage, you include experience within your mind of metta.

165
00:11:43,848 --> 00:11:47,533
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't beam your mind of metta towards the experience and chase it down.

166
00:11:49,341 --> 00:12:03,328
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize that this mind of friendliness, it's more general than anything, any particular person, any particular experience that can come my way, be it from north, be it from south, be it from the west, be it from the east, be it from up, be it from down.

167
00:12:04,028 --> 00:12:08,771
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recognizing that, you get to include all these directions within your mind of friendliness.

168
00:12:09,712 --> 00:12:12,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like send your mind of friendliness down these directions.

169
00:12:12,854 --> 00:12:16,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you send it down north, you can't send it down east, west and south.

170
00:12:17,276 --> 00:12:18,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're trying to deal with this.

171
00:12:18,917 --> 00:12:19,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

172
00:12:20,018 --> 00:12:22,419
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about dealing with specific anger or specific issues.

173
00:12:22,439 --> 00:12:29,243
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about including any possibility for anger or issue within the unchanging mind of friendliness.

174
00:12:31,447 --> 00:12:35,869
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he includes the left, he includes the right, the front, the back, up and down.

175
00:12:36,269 --> 00:12:37,950
[SPEAKER_00]: He includes it all within the framework.

176
00:12:37,990 --> 00:12:39,611
[SPEAKER_00]: He establishes the right order.

177
00:12:39,811 --> 00:12:41,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Framework first, any direction second.

178
00:12:42,552 --> 00:12:51,796
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why whatever comes to that direction, good, bad, neutral, pleasant food, unpleasant food, pleasant people, unpleasant people, will not perturb the mind, the friendliness.

179
00:12:51,876 --> 00:12:55,858
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's called immeasurable, not tied to any particular experience.

180
00:12:57,725 --> 00:12:59,487
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not fighting against anything.

181
00:12:59,647 --> 00:13:00,388
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

182
00:13:00,408 --> 00:13:00,968
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the opposite.

183
00:13:01,469 --> 00:13:02,029
[SPEAKER_00]: It's non-contention.

184
00:13:02,049 --> 00:13:03,371
[SPEAKER_00]: It's overcoming the fight, yeah.

185
00:13:05,312 --> 00:13:08,215
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not coveting the pleasure of any kind.

186
00:13:08,455 --> 00:13:09,717
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

187
00:13:09,737 --> 00:13:13,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Or trying to push away this and that.

188
00:13:13,020 --> 00:13:18,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, so it comes down again to feelings, that... to be able to see your feelings.

189
00:13:19,628 --> 00:13:23,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, yeah, because that's that's the measure of experience for everyone.

190
00:13:23,171 --> 00:13:26,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether experience is good or bad is whether it's felt pleasantly or unpleasantly.

191
00:13:26,915 --> 00:13:27,756
[SPEAKER_00]: And everything's felt.

192
00:13:28,036 --> 00:13:44,192
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by discerning that the mind of friendliness can remain unchanged, regardless of the pleasant or unpleasant experience, basically, you're training your mind to remain economist regardless of how you feel, how you particularly feel.

193
00:13:46,608 --> 00:13:56,333
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, whether you're developing the principle of friendliness and metta, or principle of jhāna, the mind is developed in the same way.

194
00:13:56,353 --> 00:14:06,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this thing, being mindful of feelings, and then developing that benevolence towards feelings,

195
00:14:10,123 --> 00:14:15,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the only criteria for pleasant, unpleasant, agreeable, disagreeable, it's whether how it's felt.

196
00:14:15,790 --> 00:14:28,765
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if, you know, the unpleasant person is unpleasant because you feel unpleasant on account of the experience of that person, not because that unpleasantness is contained chemically in them or something like that.

197
00:14:30,078 --> 00:14:34,919
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, on another occasion, when your mood is good, the same unpleasant person, you don't feel unpleasant about it.

198
00:14:35,399 --> 00:14:40,721
[SPEAKER_00]: If the unpleasantness were truly in people or things, it would always be felt the same way.

199
00:14:40,741 --> 00:14:43,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like the same thing is sometimes felt like this, sometimes like that.

200
00:14:43,981 --> 00:14:44,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you recognize.

201
00:14:44,962 --> 00:14:48,062
[SPEAKER_00]: That unpleasantness is basically determined how you feel at a time.

202
00:14:48,703 --> 00:14:58,825
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you train your mind to be imperturbable in regard to how you feel at any time, there's absolutely nothing from any direction that can disturb that, because it's all second to it.

203
00:15:01,790 --> 00:15:10,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, the cultivation of peace, which is Brahma-vihāra, that peace is in regard to anything.

204
00:15:10,332 --> 00:15:11,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yes, yeah.

205
00:15:11,292 --> 00:15:13,513
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not peace because I gain pleasure.

206
00:15:13,553 --> 00:15:14,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, no.

207
00:15:14,293 --> 00:15:16,674
[SPEAKER_01]: It's peace because it's undisturbed.

208
00:15:17,674 --> 00:15:18,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Regardless of what is happening.

209
00:15:19,274 --> 00:15:20,995
[SPEAKER_00]: It's peace because it's undisturbed, yeah.

210
00:15:34,139 --> 00:15:40,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So that cultivating one who trains in Brahma-vihāra, in the suttas,

211
00:15:42,641 --> 00:15:55,925
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't hear, like, he trains in Brahmavihāra and then he goes out to the village to teach, to do charitable work, to start a garden, you know, teach people how to farm, how to fish.

212
00:15:56,425 --> 00:15:57,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

213
00:15:57,105 --> 00:15:57,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Educate them.

214
00:15:57,705 --> 00:15:58,425
[SPEAKER_00]: No, of course not.

215
00:15:58,585 --> 00:16:01,806
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't become a psychotherapist and deal with the traumas of people.

216
00:16:01,826 --> 00:16:03,607
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because that's all management.

217
00:16:03,647 --> 00:16:04,907
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all tying yourself down.

218
00:16:05,813 --> 00:16:12,738
[SPEAKER_01]: But when he cult... he develops brahmavihāra, there's no... there's nothing about that he goes out and does outreach programs.

219
00:16:13,198 --> 00:16:13,718
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

220
00:16:15,019 --> 00:16:15,660
[SPEAKER_01]: He does nothing.

221
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:17,901
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because that's not what brahmavihāras are.

222
00:16:18,442 --> 00:16:23,825
[SPEAKER_01]: And it says he doesn't crave for things, he doesn't covet things, he doesn't know your will.

223
00:16:24,205 --> 00:16:24,926
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all.

224
00:16:24,946 --> 00:16:25,306
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

225
00:16:25,606 --> 00:16:28,789
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's your greatest contribution to the world, being incapable of your will.

226
00:16:29,689 --> 00:16:30,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

227
00:16:30,310 --> 00:16:31,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Not spreading love.

228
00:16:31,370 --> 00:16:33,872
[SPEAKER_01]: But then...

229
00:16:36,046 --> 00:16:37,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have love without passion?

230
00:16:38,948 --> 00:16:40,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have ill-will without passion?

231
00:16:41,150 --> 00:16:41,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

232
00:16:43,331 --> 00:16:48,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you have ignorance, delusion, obstinacy, distraction, without passion?

233
00:16:48,775 --> 00:16:49,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

234
00:16:50,195 --> 00:16:54,078
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you remove the passion, how can you love anyone?

235
00:16:54,979 --> 00:16:55,939
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you hate anyone?

236
00:16:57,120 --> 00:16:57,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

237
00:16:59,061 --> 00:17:00,282
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the passion is a problem.

238
00:17:03,891 --> 00:17:08,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Passion is the root of all greed, all good and all bad.

239
00:17:08,033 --> 00:17:11,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, because of passion, all good and bad are still tying you down.

240
00:17:11,955 --> 00:17:19,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter if it's just, you know, good is better than bad, but from the ultimate point of view, if you want to be free from it all, it's equally bad.

241
00:17:20,139 --> 00:17:20,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

242
00:17:22,220 --> 00:17:23,961
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you would think, well,

243
00:17:25,523 --> 00:17:26,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Generosity.

244
00:17:26,343 --> 00:17:31,005
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I want to practice brahma viharas, so I must actually go out and be generous.

245
00:17:32,145 --> 00:17:33,926
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's... there's two different things.

246
00:17:34,126 --> 00:17:34,946
[SPEAKER_00]: It is, it is, yeah.

247
00:17:35,466 --> 00:17:41,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Generosity can help, you know, sort out your circumstances, you know, people will like you.

248
00:17:41,248 --> 00:17:46,970
[SPEAKER_00]: You practice generosity because it will make you feel good, which means practically you're acting out of the wanting to feel good.

249
00:17:47,631 --> 00:17:51,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, some people who don't have any sense of generosity,

250
00:17:52,416 --> 00:17:55,038
[SPEAKER_00]: They should, maybe, at least, do that.

251
00:17:55,318 --> 00:17:56,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Go through the motion of generosity.

252
00:17:57,619 --> 00:18:07,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Just to kind of... You know, in the same sense, like, if somebody is too loose, tightening up your restraint a bit more, to the point of, you know, being a bit painful, it's a good thing.

253
00:18:08,626 --> 00:18:14,270
[SPEAKER_00]: But if somebody is too tight, like losing their mind on account of tightness, then loosening up is good for them.

254
00:18:15,111 --> 00:18:18,613
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not always to say that loosening is always good or tightening is always good.

255
00:18:19,595 --> 00:18:33,830
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for somebody who is not generous, who doesn't... who doesn't, like, even have that notion, or any recognition of pleasantness of sharing, they should make the effort to develop it, by giving, literally.

256
00:18:36,197 --> 00:18:42,561
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it's important to not fall into a view that giving and generosity is somehow making you closer to nibbāna.

257
00:18:42,661 --> 00:18:43,041
[SPEAKER_00]: It isn't.

258
00:18:43,681 --> 00:18:44,282
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

259
00:18:44,662 --> 00:18:49,925
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you must replace bad, which is non-giving, stealing and so on, with good.

260
00:18:50,745 --> 00:18:53,287
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't mistake that replacing bad with good

261
00:18:54,323 --> 00:18:55,985
[SPEAKER_00]: is the practice of nibbāna, because it isn't.

262
00:18:56,565 --> 00:19:01,370
[SPEAKER_00]: It can enable you to practice nibbāna better, but in itself it isn't.

263
00:19:01,410 --> 00:19:03,392
[SPEAKER_01]: So, generosity is the...

264
00:19:03,452 --> 00:19:04,513
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the level of virtue.

265
00:19:04,653 --> 00:19:04,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

266
00:19:05,113 --> 00:19:05,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Virtue.

267
00:19:05,734 --> 00:19:06,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtues be generous.

268
00:19:07,075 --> 00:19:08,957
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not the development of nibbāna.

269
00:19:09,117 --> 00:19:09,257
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

270
00:19:09,277 --> 00:19:10,118
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not for the practice.

271
00:19:10,338 --> 00:19:14,282
[SPEAKER_00]: It creates a basis, but that basis in itself is insufficient.

272
00:19:14,462 --> 00:19:17,145
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a basis if there is nothing placed on that basis.

273
00:19:17,885 --> 00:19:21,448
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, there's brahma-vihāra practice and there's generosity.

274
00:19:22,209 --> 00:19:22,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

275
00:19:22,429 --> 00:19:23,190
[SPEAKER_00]: There are two different things.

276
00:19:23,751 --> 00:19:23,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

277
00:19:24,011 --> 00:19:24,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Two different things.

278
00:19:24,611 --> 00:19:28,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you don't practice brahma-vihāra by being generous.

279
00:19:28,295 --> 00:19:32,458
[SPEAKER_00]: You can develop brahma-vihāras on account of being generous beforehand.

280
00:19:32,819 --> 00:19:36,402
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, you can develop jhāna on account of being virtuous beforehand.

281
00:19:38,516 --> 00:19:40,898
[SPEAKER_01]: It brings a possibility for those things.

282
00:19:40,938 --> 00:19:41,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

283
00:19:41,858 --> 00:19:42,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtue, generosity.

284
00:19:44,280 --> 00:19:47,342
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't automatically give you insight.

285
00:19:47,382 --> 00:19:47,922
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

286
00:19:48,062 --> 00:19:49,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Nibbāna, parinibbāna, there we go.

287
00:19:50,824 --> 00:19:56,788
[SPEAKER_01]: So, brahmavihāra is the practice of nibbāna.

288
00:19:57,288 --> 00:19:57,589
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

289
00:19:57,709 --> 00:19:59,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Practice of dispassion, practice of

290
00:20:01,756 --> 00:20:10,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Inperturbability, practice of remaining unaffected, that's why the ultimate, the highest brahmavihāra is upekha, equanimity, indifference.

291
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:21,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you practice your friendliness rightly, you will become perfectly indifferent to everything, pain and pleasure, in yourself, let alone out there in the world.

292
00:20:36,745 --> 00:20:38,386
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha continues.

293
00:20:38,887 --> 00:20:53,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, Jivaka says, I've heard this, Venerable Sir, Brahma abides in benevolence, compassion, contentment, equanimity.

294
00:20:53,178 --> 00:20:55,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Vomita, karuṇa, mudita, upeka.

295
00:21:01,238 --> 00:21:08,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Venerable Sir, the Blessed One is my visible witness to that, for the Blessed One abides in those same qualities.

296
00:21:10,095 --> 00:21:11,919
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the Buddha says,

297
00:21:14,260 --> 00:21:31,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Jivaka, any lust, any hate, any delusion, whereby ill will, cruelty, or discontent, or aversion might arise, have been abandoned by the targeter, cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, done away with so that they are no longer subject to future arising.

298
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:35,541
[SPEAKER_01]: If what you said refer to that, then I allow it to you.

299
00:21:36,181 --> 00:21:37,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, that's what I mean.

300
00:21:37,341 --> 00:21:39,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is... That's what Brahma has asked.

301
00:21:39,461 --> 00:21:40,101
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it is.

302
00:21:40,541 --> 00:21:42,102
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a complete dispassion.

303
00:21:42,842 --> 00:21:47,743
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no outreach programs, doing this, doing this, putting a smile on your face.

304
00:21:47,763 --> 00:21:51,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Or spreading your love to the left and to the right and loving everyone.

305
00:21:51,644 --> 00:21:51,804
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

306
00:21:52,044 --> 00:21:58,045
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, you cannot possibly love anyone, love someone and be emotionally indifferent towards them.

307
00:21:58,205 --> 00:21:59,445
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a contradiction in terms.

308
00:22:00,090 --> 00:22:02,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, the cultivation of brahmavihāra is that.

309
00:22:02,571 --> 00:22:05,873
[SPEAKER_00]: This passionate, emotional, imperturbability, indifference.

310
00:22:07,793 --> 00:22:10,074
[SPEAKER_00]: And you cannot arrive at that by spreading love.

311
00:22:11,315 --> 00:22:14,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it contradicts the very principle of that matter.

312
00:22:17,558 --> 00:22:19,379
[SPEAKER_00]: You can arrive at that by practicing non-hate.

313
00:22:21,059 --> 00:22:24,701
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's much more subtle than just visualizing love, universal love, to everyone.

314
00:22:33,908 --> 00:22:37,790
[SPEAKER_00]: And even that, whether you love somebody or not, it's determined how you feel in regard to them.

315
00:22:38,951 --> 00:22:46,534
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you practice indifference towards your own feeling, you become indifferent, by proxy, to anyone you love or anyone you hate.

316
00:22:47,275 --> 00:22:48,635
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you overcome all of that.

317
00:22:48,796 --> 00:22:49,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

318
00:22:49,376 --> 00:22:53,238
[SPEAKER_01]: So, someone might... you might not... someone might make you feel uncomfortable.

319
00:22:53,798 --> 00:22:54,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

320
00:22:54,158 --> 00:22:55,759
[SPEAKER_00]: That will not be a basis for your hate.

321
00:22:55,839 --> 00:23:03,363
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no... Somebody might make you feel comfortable, safe, agreeable, but there's no base for love and infatuation.

322
00:23:15,414 --> 00:23:21,639
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah.

323
00:23:21,919 --> 00:23:27,243
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you were saying the other day, the framework, a bit of the framework of friendliness.

324
00:23:29,065 --> 00:23:30,546
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you said.

325
00:23:30,626 --> 00:23:32,067
[SPEAKER_00]: You abide in the qualities.

326
00:23:33,068 --> 00:23:34,909
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you discern the quality of friendliness.

327
00:23:34,929 --> 00:23:37,712
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes a general experience.

328
00:23:37,792 --> 00:23:44,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the quality of friendliness that I dwell on, you establish your mind upon that context of non-ill-will, non-harm.

329
00:23:46,762 --> 00:23:51,146
[SPEAKER_00]: So, abiding in the quality of friendliness means developing the context of metta.

330
00:23:53,848 --> 00:24:00,253
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can only do that on the level of your mind, not on the level of chasing down everybody you hate or don't love enough.

331
00:24:04,977 --> 00:24:05,737
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you make...

332
00:24:06,318 --> 00:24:10,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to be able to recognize those qualities?

333
00:24:10,081 --> 00:24:10,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

334
00:24:10,922 --> 00:24:11,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Good.

335
00:24:11,522 --> 00:24:13,924
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to develop that context of non-ill will.

336
00:24:14,264 --> 00:24:14,565
[SPEAKER_00]: And then?

337
00:24:15,408 --> 00:24:23,671
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, once you start developing the context of knowing, then you start to recognize that doesn't matter from which direction things come at me, they all have to converge on how I feel about them.

338
00:24:24,291 --> 00:24:28,732
[SPEAKER_00]: In my mind, practice is the context of indifference or friendliness towards my own feeling.

339
00:24:29,473 --> 00:24:34,974
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're friendly towards your own feeling, you will not resist it, you will not indulge it, you will not try to get rid of it, you will not try to replace it.

340
00:24:35,154 --> 00:24:36,975
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be unmoved by it.

341
00:24:40,318 --> 00:24:40,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

342
00:24:41,158 --> 00:24:45,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever something's contacting you, this is, yeah, peaceful.

343
00:24:45,600 --> 00:24:48,540
[SPEAKER_00]: The common view is you practice metta towards yourself, then you spread it to others.

344
00:24:49,881 --> 00:25:00,144
[SPEAKER_00]: It's true in as much you practice metta towards how you feel, in regard to anything, and then you include even experience of others within that.

345
00:25:00,464 --> 00:25:04,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no experience of others without you feeling something on account of experience of others.

346
00:25:07,025 --> 00:25:11,489
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by practicing mantra correctly, you only practice it to yourself, because it's not a way.

347
00:25:13,350 --> 00:25:20,256
[SPEAKER_00]: And you spread it to others by basically including others within what you already cultivate towards your own feeling.

348
00:25:23,419 --> 00:25:28,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Not at the expense of, okay, I finished practicing towards myself, now let me do it towards others.

349
00:25:28,944 --> 00:25:34,709
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's all tied to this, and then to that, and then to both, and then to neither, and then left and right, and you have no idea.

350
00:25:36,351 --> 00:25:48,635
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you develop the context correctly, context should remain unchanged, regardless whether it's you, left, right, north, east, south, doesn't matter, the framework remains of non-ill-will.

351
00:25:49,155 --> 00:25:57,557
[SPEAKER_00]: And that framework is rooted in you being unmoved towards whatever you're currently feeling, good, bad or neutral, whichever of those things might be present.

352
00:25:58,718 --> 00:26:06,080
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you always have the attitude of friendliness towards how you're feeling, can you experience any thirst and lust and passion?

353
00:26:06,895 --> 00:26:07,035
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

354
00:26:07,575 --> 00:26:09,615
[SPEAKER_00]: That implies you want to change what you're feeling.

355
00:26:09,635 --> 00:26:10,496
[SPEAKER_00]: You want more of it.

356
00:26:12,976 --> 00:26:19,357
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're practicing indifference towards how you're feeling, can you then act out of trying to get rid of the unpleasantness that you might be feeling?

357
00:26:19,377 --> 00:26:19,597
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

358
00:26:19,937 --> 00:26:21,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that also implies you want to change, get rid of it.

359
00:26:21,998 --> 00:26:23,418
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you're not friendly towards it.

360
00:26:23,558 --> 00:26:24,278
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not peaceful.

361
00:26:24,418 --> 00:26:25,158
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not peaceful.

362
00:26:25,218 --> 00:26:26,258
[SPEAKER_00]: You are moved by it.

363
00:26:29,379 --> 00:26:32,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And you get moved by it by acting out of it in the first place.

364
00:26:33,239 --> 00:26:34,600
[SPEAKER_00]: You move yourself out of it.

365
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:36,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Feelings cannot move you.

366
00:26:37,310 --> 00:26:39,491
[SPEAKER_00]: You move yourself on account of what you feel.

367
00:26:46,876 --> 00:26:53,560
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you practice metta, friendliness towards whatever currently feeling is enduring, you practice metta towards the whole world.

368
00:26:54,281 --> 00:26:56,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Because feelings are always convergent point.

369
00:26:56,722 --> 00:27:01,465
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot experience the world without... unless through your own feelings in regard to it.

370
00:27:03,287 --> 00:27:03,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

371
00:27:08,393 --> 00:27:09,734
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you cover the whole world.

372
00:27:09,834 --> 00:27:13,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's how you include the whole world, as you translated initially.

373
00:27:13,875 --> 00:27:21,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you just translate how you spread and stuff, but the same word can mean you include, you infer, you bring it in.

